.TH SASH-CONVERT 1 "2026-11-12" "safebash" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sash-convert \- translate Bash source into a safebash starting point
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sash-convert
.RB [ \-\-annotate ]
.RB [ "\-o " output.sb ]
.RI [ input.bash ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sash-convert
rewrites supported Bash constructs into safebash source and adds guidance
where human review is needed.
It is a separate source-conversion tool, not a mode of
.BR sash (1).
It does not execute the input, compile the output, or prove that the two
programs are equivalent.
.PP
With no input filename, it reads standard input.
Otherwise it reads the named file.
Input must be UTF-8.
Output goes to standard output unless
.B \-o
is supplied.
The recommended suffix for generated safebash source is
.BR .sb ;
the converter does not choose an output filename automatically.
.PP
Conversion is a starting point for editing.
Unsupported constructs may remain in the output with annotations.
Even an exit status of 0 does not mean that
.B sash
will accept the result or that generated validation predicates suit
the application.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-annotate
Add
.B "# was:"
comments showing the original text of mechanical rewrites.
Manual-review annotations are produced independently of this option.
See BUGS before using annotated output.
.TP
.BI "\-o " output.sb
Write to the named file instead of standard output.
An existing file is replaced; no backup is made.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print usage to standard error and exit 0.
.PP
These are the supported options.
There is no
.BR \-\-emit-source ,
.BR \-\-nolint ,
or
.B \-\-version
option.
Neither
.B \-
as a stdin alias nor
.B \-\-
as an option terminator is implemented.
Omit the input filename to read stdin; prefix a dash-leading filename
with a directory such as
.BR ./ .
.SH REVIEW ANNOTATIONS
.TP
.B "# MANUAL:"
An unsupported form or author decision needs manual conversion.
.TP
.B "# CHECK:"
A possible semantic change needs review, such as Bash word splitting
that safebash will not perform.
.TP
.B "# ATTEST:"
Acquired data needs application-specific validation.
.TP
.B "# was:"
Original text associated with a rewrite, requested by
.BR \-\-annotate .
.PP
Some recognized external commands produce actual
.B attest
statements rather than hints.
Other rewrites can introduce placeholder predicates with a request to
tighten them.
Review both the comments and executable validation: a generated predicate
is not blanket approval of the data or its intended use.
.SH SHELLCHECK
The converter attempts to run
.B shellcheck
from
.B PATH
with JSON output, all optional checks enabled, and Bash syntax selected.
The source is passed to ShellCheck on standard input.
Findings can inform conversion and annotations.
.PP
ShellCheck is optional analysis, not a compiler-validation gate.
If it cannot be started, the converter warns and continues without its
findings.
Do not interpret successful conversion as proof that ShellCheck ran or
reported a clean program.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Conversion and output completed, or help was requested.
The generated program can still require manual changes.
.TP
.B 1
An invocation, input or output error was reported.
.SH EXAMPLES
Given
.BR input.bash :
.PP
.EX
#!/bin/bash
declare \-i count=3
echo "$count"
.EE
.PP
Convert, review the generated file, then compile it:
.PP
.EX
sash-convert \-o program.sb input.bash
sash program.sb \-o program
\&./program
.EE
.PP
The program prints
.BR 3 .
Compilation uses the file, not a pipe into the compiler.
.PP
Read Bash source from stdin and retain rewrite annotations for review:
.PP
.EX
sash-convert \-\-annotate < input.bash > review.sb
.EE
.SH BUGS
Some
.B \-\-annotate
rewrites insert
.B "# was:"
mid-line.
The comment can hide later code or become part of a string, changing
the generated program.
Review comment placement; successful conversion does not detect this defect.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sash (1),
.BR sash-getopt (1),
.BR sash-expand (1),
.BR shellcheck (1)
